    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country validation of the E.164 national-number length. When a telephone's country calling code is recognized, the national portion must match that country's required digit count \u2014 `+61` requires 9 national digits, `+1` requires 10 \u2014 otherwise the request is rejected with `400 Bad Request`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `E164CountryRules` component** (`rest/controller/E164CountryRules.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility class that owns the calling-code \u2192 required-national-length mapping (`61 \u2192 9`, `1 \u2192 10`). Given the digit string after the `+`, it strips the longest matching calling code and checks the remaining national digits against that country's requirement. Unrecognized calling codes carry no per-country rule and pass through unchanged.\n\n2. **`TelephoneNormalizer`** \u2014 now delegates to `E164CountryRules.validateNationalLength(...)` after its existing 8\u201315 total-digit sanity check, keeping its role as the E.164 normalization orchestrator without absorbing country-specific knowledge.\n\n3. **`InvalidTelephoneException`** \u2014 replaced the single message constructor with two clear static factories: `notE164(...)` for the generic format failure and `wrongNationalLength(...)` for the per-country length failure, so each rejection carries an accurate message. Both still surface as `400` via the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` handler.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posting `+61 123`) passes, and the existing valid-owner flows \u2014 including the AU `04XXXXXXXX` numbers that normalize to `+61` with 9 national digits \u2014 continue to work.",
